No design system exists in the repo (src/ is being built by another node; web/ is empty). So I'm establishing the design system in style.css with CSS custom properties as tokens. The brief gives the palette: paper #faf6ef, forest green #1f4d3a, terracotta #c0392b, Georgia serif headings. I'll build a token set:

- Colors: --paper, --ink (deep warm dark for text), --primary (#1f4d3a), --accent (#c0392b), plus derived tints: --primary-soft, --line (border color), --card surface white-ish.
- Spacing scale: 4px base: --s-1:4px ... --s-8:64px
- Radii: --r-sm 6px, --r-md 10px, --r-lg 16px (rounded-xl ~16px)
- Shadows: soft layered
- Transition: --t-fast 160ms ease

Typography: Georgia stack for display ("Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif"), body maybe system serif/sans mix — the direction says "serif display headings", so body can be a humanist sans or also serif; warm feel suggests body in a readable sans like -apple-system stack but avoid generic? Design quality says avoid Arial/Inter/Roboto as *distinctive* fonts... For vanilla CSS without webfonts (no build step, should work offline on Workers assets — could use Google Fonts link though). A Google Fonts link is fine (no build step needed, just a <link>). But offline environments would fall back. I'll use a font stack with graceful fallback: display: Georgia stack per spec (mandated). Body: use a serif-leaning humanist stack too for warmth — e.g. "Avenir Next", "Segoe UI"... Actually the design mandate says avoid generic fonts, but the product spec explicitly mandates Georgia stack for display headings — follow the spec (it's authoritative). For body text I'll use a clean stack: `"Gill Sans", "Segoe UI", "Trebuchet MS", sans-serif`? Hmm, keep it tasteful: body in Georgia-family serif can look very warm/paper-like and cohesive for a family-tree heritage vibe. I'll go: display Georgia bold; body `Georgia, 'Iowan Old Style', 'Palatino Linotype', serif` for narrative bits and a small-caps label treatment using letter-spacing. That gives a distinctive archival/genealogy aesthetic consistent with the theme.

Now the app itself. Requirements recap:

**Routes (hash router):**
- `#/login` — combined login/signup forms (both on one page, tabs or side-by-side).
- `#/trees` — list of my trees (GET /api/trees), create tree form (POST /api/trees {name}).
- `#/tree/<id>` — tree view.

**Auth flow:** fetch-based client with same-origin cookies (`credentials: 'same-origin'` is default for same-origin actually; default is 'same-origin' — fine, but be explicit). Login stores nothing besides in-memory state. After login navigate to `#/trees`. On boot: call GET /api/me to know if logged in; if not logged in and route != login → redirect to #/login.

**Tree view (THE core requirement):**
- Fetch GET /api/trees/:id/generations → `{generations:[{gen,couples:[{couple_id,other_info,child_count,partners:[{person_id,name,gender,dob,dod},...]}]}]}` ordered gen asc.
- Render one horizontal band per generation, generation 1 on top. Each couple = a card (connected box) showing partner_a name left, partner_b right, heart/dot separator.
- Clicking couple box opens a SMALL RECTANGULAR BOX showing Father name, Mother name, Other info snippet. This small box — where does it appear? Probably anchored near/below the clicked couple card, or a popover. I'll render it as an anchored popover below the clicked card (or a fixed panel under the band). Simplest robust: an absolutely-positioned popover near the card within the band area, clamped to viewport. Or simpler: a dedicated "detail strip" that appears directly beneath the clicked couple card inside the band container. Given responsive constraints (360px), an inline expansion beneath the card is most robust: clicking a card toggles a small box right under it (inside the card's wrapper) showing Father / Mother / other-info snippet, with names clickable.

Actually re-read: "the tree page renders the hierarchy of COUPLE nodes as connected boxes... Clicking a couple box opens a SMALL RECTANGULAR BOX showing: Father name, Mother name, and Other information (short snippet)." So the small box appears on click of the couple box. Inline expansion under the card is clean and works at all widths. I'll do that: each couple cell contains the card + a hidden detail box that expands when selected.

"Clicking the father name or mother name INSIDE that small box opens the BIG BOX: a modal with ALL details of that person" — fetched via GET /api/persons/:pid. Modal shows name, gender, dob, dod, height, weight, address, mail, phone, job, degree, place, other info, photo (img if photo_url).

Kinship badge in big modal: GET /api/me → my person_id; then GET /api/kinship?tree=<tid>&from=<my pid>&to=<pid>. Display terms[userLang].term prominently + gloss underneath. Hide gracefully if no person_id or 404/error. userLang from /api/me (lang field presumably — contract says signup returns lang; /api/me returns current user, presumably {user_id,email,name,lang,person_id}). I'll read user.person_id and user.lang defensively.

**Connectors (SVG lines):**
"draw connectors between consecutive bands from each upper card to each lower card horizontally nearest, acceptable for v1". Also mentions deriving parent-child links: "a couple is a child-band member if its partners appear as children of the upper couple". Hmm — the generations payload only has couples with child_count per generation band. There's no explicit parent-child mapping between couples across bands! The contract's payload doesn't include which upper couple is the parent of which lower couple. So the simplest robust approach sanctioned by the task: connect each lower-band card to nearest horizontally-positioned upper-band card. I'll implement: after layout, measure DOM positions of cards in consecutive bands, draw SVG bezier/elbow lines from bottom-center of each lower card up to the bottom-center of its horizontally-nearest upper card... wait, direction: "from each couple card bottom-center to the midpoint of its children's cards". Upper card bottom-center → children midpoints. With no parent map, assign each lower card to nearest upper card; group lower cards by assigned parent; line goes parent bottom-center → down to a bus line → horizontal to above each child → down to child top-center. Classic genealogy elbow connectors. That satisfies "acceptable for v1".

Implementation: absolutely positioned SVG overlay sized to the bands container, redrawn on resize (ResizeObserver or window resize) and after render. Compute positions via getBoundingClientRect relative to container.

Edge cases:
- Band with multiple couples: lay them out horizontally centered with flex wrap? For connector math, flex-wrap complicates measurement but since we measure actual DOM rects it still works.
- Couples with partner_a/partner_b possibly missing (couple may have only one partner?). Contract: partners array; POST couples takes optional partner_a?, partner_b?. So partners array may have 0, 1, or 2 entries. Render: left slot = first partner, right slot = second; empty slots show "—". Separator dot if both present, heart? Brief says "small heart or dot separator". I'll use a small heart glyph (♥) styled terracotta.
